In the Netherlands and the European Union, return rules depend on whether you buy an item online or in a physical store.

Online Purchases (EU & Netherlands)

• 14-Day Cooling-Off Period: You have 14 days to cancel or return an online, phone, or off-premises purchase without giving a reason, starting the day after you receive the item.
• Refund Timeline: Sellers must refund the full purchase price and standard delivery costs within 14 days of your cancellation notification.
• Exceptions: Custom-made items, perishable goods, hygiene products with broken seals, and sealed audio/video software or tickets are excluded.
• Return Costs: Sellers must inform you beforehand if you have to pay for return shipping; otherwise, the seller covers it.

In-Store Purchases (Netherlands)

• No Legal Right to Return: Physical shops in the Netherlands are not legally required to accept returns or give refunds for buyer's remorse (change of mind).
• Discretionary Policies: If a physical store accepts a return, they may offer an exchange, a gift card, or store credit instead of cash.
• According to a consensus on Reddit, local shops handle non-faulty in-store returns purely by their own internal store rules.

Defective or Faulty Goods (Legal Guarantee)

• 2-Year Protection: Across the EU, you have a minimum 2-year legal guarantee for items that are broken, faulty, or do not match what was advertised.
• Remedies: The seller must first repair or replace the item for free. If that is impossible or fails, you can receive a partial or full refund.
